# SYNOPSIS

```swift
enum Port {
  static func create() throws(Status) -> Handle
  static func create(bindToInterrupt: Bool) throws(Status) -> Handle

  @discardableResult
  static func bind(_ port: borrowing Handle, _ items: inout [Binding])
    throws(Status) -> Int
  static func bind(_ port: borrowing Handle, _ item: Binding)
    throws(Status)

  @discardableResult
  static func queue(_ port: borrowing Handle, _ packets: [Packet])
    throws(Status) -> Int
  static func queue(_ port: borrowing Handle, _ packet: Packet)
    throws(Status)

  static func wait(_ port: borrowing Handle,
                   deadline: Int64 = infiniteDeadline, max: Int)
    throws(Status) -> [Packet]
  static func wait(_ port: borrowing Handle,
                   deadline: Int64 = infiniteDeadline)
    throws(Status) -> Packet

  static func cancel(_ port: borrowing Handle, source: borrowing Handle,
                     key: UInt64) throws(Status)
  static func cancel(_ port: borrowing Handle, key: UInt64) throws(Status)
}
```

# DESCRIPTION

A port is a queue of packets.
Packets come to it from user space, with `queue`, and from objects bound to it: a channel that becomes readable, a process that ends, an interrupt, a counter that reaches a value.
Waiting for an object is waiting on a port: `Handle.wait` and `sleep(until:)` do it on the calling thread's own port.
A packet (`Packet`) has a key, a type, a status and a payload of 32 bytes.

`create()` returns a new port.
`create(bindToInterrupt: true)` returns a port that interrupt objects may be bound to.
It is `port_create(2)`.

`bind` binds objects to `port` as `object_bind(2)` does.
Each `Binding` names an object, the signals or value or interrupt to watch, and the key its packets carry.
With an array, `bind` binds the items in order, stops at the first that fails, writes each item's status back, and returns how many were bound.
With one item, it throws that item's status if the bind failed.
`Handle.bind` and `Interrupt.bind` build the binding for the common cases.

`queue` puts user packets on the port in order.
It stops at the first that cannot go, which is `shouldWait` when the port holds too many, and returns how many were queued.
With one packet it throws on failure.
It is `port_queue(2)`.

`wait` returns the next packet, or with `max` up to `max` packets, at least 1 and at most 64.
It waits until the monotonic `deadline` (see sys-clock(2)) for the first and throws `timedOut` then; the others it returns are those already queued.
It is `port_wait(2)`.

`cancel` undoes the bindings to `port` with `key` that were made through `source`, and drops their queued packets.
Without `source` it undoes every binding with `key` and drops every queued packet with it, user packets too.
It throws `notFound` if there were none.
It is `port_cancel(2)`.

